<?xml version="1.0"?>
<robot name="vision_arm" xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">

  <!-- ponytail: camera defaults OFF - this VM has no GPU and llvmpipe takes
       >45s per RGBD frame, which lockstep-stalls physics to RTF 0.006 (sim
       unusable). Enable with xacro arg camera:=true on a GPU box / VBox with
       3D accel when Phase 5 vision needs it; camera_link TF frames exist
       either way, so downstream code can be written against them now.
       Declared HERE, before the gripper macro is expanded below - gripper.xacro
       reads $(arg camera) to gate the camera's visual, and xacro resolves args
       in document order, so declaring it further down silently breaks. -->
  <xacro:arg name="camera" default="false"/>

  <xacro:include filename="$(find vision_arm_description)/urdf/gripper.xacro"/>

  <!-- Joint limits derived from Software1/Low_Level_Arduino/Robot_Arduino_trajectory/
       Robot_Arduino_trajectory.ino: meh_limit[] (mechanical step limits from the
       limit switch) * step_angle[] (deg/full-step, J3 motor is 0.9 deg, rest 1.8 deg)
       * microstep[] (1/8, 1/8, 1/4, 1/2, 1/2, 1/2) / gear_ratio[] (docs/B_Design_decisions.rst).
       homeAll() does NOT leave every joint's current_position at 0 after homing -
       J1/J4/J5/J6 are re-zeroed to a home offset (10000/2275/1173/3838 steps) partway
       through their mechanical travel, and CAD zero (URDF joint value 0) corresponds to
       that homed pose, not to the limit switch. Limits below are recentered so lower/upper
       bracket the true travel on each side of home (span = meh_limit * step_angle *
       microstep / gear_ratio, unchanged); J2/J3 home at their limit switch (offset 0) so
       their range is still [0, upper]. Verify sign/direction against real hardware in Phase 8.
       velocity/effort are conservative placeholders, not measured stepper torque curves. -->
  <xacro:property name="vel_limit" value="1.0"/>
  <!-- 5.0 N*m (the original placeholder) starved joint2_shoulder in Gazebo: gravity
       alone needs ~28 N*m at the output shaft to hold ~5.7kg of downstream arm+gripper
       at a ~0.5m moment arm (mass*g*arm), confirmed by controller_state showing
       "reference" climbing normally while "feedback" stayed pinned at zero under real
       physics. Bumped with margin; still a placeholder pending measured stepper+
       gearbox torque curves (cycloidal 15-27:1 reductions make tens of N*m at the
       output plausible from a small stepper). -->
  <xacro:property name="eff_limit" value="50.0"/>

  <!-- The base is BOLTED to the work surface: a "world" link plus a fixed
       world_joint at mount_z. On real hardware the arm is clamped to a bench, so
       this is also the honest model.

       Why the free-floating base was abandoned: joint1 only turned because the
       base counter-rotated under it (commanding -0.3 yawed the base +0.181, -0.9
       yawed it +0.460 - about half the commanded angle is absorbed). That is
       survivable for perception, which measures cubes in base_link and rides the
       base with them, but it is fatal for pick and place: the CUBES are bolted to
       the world, not to the base, so a cube measured at a base_link coordinate is
       no longer at that coordinate once the base has yawed 26 degrees mid-move.

       mount_z: base_link's collision box underside sits 0.0157 above base_link's
       origin, so mount_z = (work surface height) - 0.0157 puts the plate exactly
       on the surface. 0.75 m is the workbench top defined a few lines below; a test
       in test_urdf.py asserts the two stay in sync.

       An earlier attempt at this weld (2026-08-12) left joint1 DEAD in Gazebo -
       commanded -1.213, reached 0.0007, while joints 2-6 tracked to 1e-12, and
       it stayed dead with joint1's effort raised 50 -> 500 N*m, so it was never
       effort starvation. That is fixed and the weld is now VERIFIED, measured
       2026-08-13 with tools/weld_check.py: commanding joint1 alone to -0.9
       reached -0.8978, and a six-joint move to [-0.6, 0.9, 0.6, 0, -0.5, 0]
       tracked every joint to within 0.003 rad, with `gz model -m vision_arm -p`
       reporting the model at exactly world identity throughout. The difference
       is almost certainly base_link's inertial origin, which used to lie outside
       its own mesh and outside its support polygon (see the note below) - the
       weld itself converts cleanly to SDF, joint1 keeps the right axis, parent
       and limits, and self_collide is off.

       Do NOT re-try these, all measured 2026-08-12 and rejected: base mass x100
       (joint1 dead at 50 N*m; at 500 N*m it tracks but the base still yaws 0.569
       rad), base mass x1000 (yaw 0 but ~10 mm of creep per move), and mu=1
       friction on base and ground (changed drift by <1 mm - the slide was
       contact-solver drift, not Coulomb sliding). -->
  <xacro:arg name="mount_z" default="0.7343"/>

  <link name="world"/>
  <joint name="world_joint" type="fixed">
    <parent link="world"/>
    <child link="base_link"/>
    <origin xyz="0 0 $(arg mount_z)" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  </joint>

  <!-- ponytail: the workbench lives HERE, not in the world SDF, so Gazebo and
       MoveIt get the same geometry from one number. The arm is bolted to this
       top, so a planner that cannot see it will plan straight through it, and
       the alternative (an SDF model plus a planning-scene publisher node) is two
       representations to keep in sync. Welded to world through fixed joints, so
       its inertia is irrelevant - the 30 kg is a plausible placeholder.
       Colour is desaturated on purpose: at S=51/255 it sits under the cube
       detector's saturation floor of 120, so it can never be read as a red cube
       (a saturated wood brown lands at hue 15, only 5 off the red band). -->
  <material name="bench_top"><color rgba="0.50 0.45 0.40 1"/></material>
  <link name="workbench">
    <inertial>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <mass value="30.0"/>
      <inertia ixx="3.906" ixy="0" ixz="0" iyy="6.306" iyz="0" izz="7.400"/>
    </inertial>
    <visual>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry><box size="1.4 1.0 0.75"/></geometry>
      <material name="bench_top"/>
    </visual>
    <collision>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry><box size="1.4 1.0 0.75"/></geometry>
    </collision>
  </link>
  <!-- Top at z=0.75, spanning x -0.55..+0.85, y -0.85..+0.15. That contains the
       whole reachable top-down grasp patch measured by tools/reach_map.py
       (x -0.31..+0.48, y -0.63..-0.19 in base_link, and base_link is at the
       world origin in x/y) with room left over, so objects can be placed
       anywhere the arm can reach without hanging off an edge. -->
  <joint name="workbench_joint" type="fixed">
    <parent link="world"/>
    <child link="workbench"/>
    <origin xyz="0.15 -0.35 0.375" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  </joint>

  <!-- Deliberately achromatic. The cube detector thresholds HSV with a
       saturation floor of 120/255, so any grey the arm is painted is invisible
       to it and cannot produce false detections; an orange or red arm could.
       Light and dark alternate link by link so the joints read as outlines
       rather than one continuous silhouette. -->
  <!-- sdformat multiplies URDF colour by 1.25 for the SDF diffuse, so anything
       above 0.8 clamps to pure white in Gazebo. 0.72 lands at 0.9. -->
  <material name="arm_body"><color rgba="0.70 0.72 0.75 1"/></material>
  <material name="arm_joint"><color rgba="0.16 0.17 0.19 1"/></material>

  <link name="base_link">
    <inertial>
      <!-- CAD gave xyz="0.0754452 0.0706654 0.1747161", which is OUTSIDE the part:
           base_link.STL spans y [-0.2967, -0.0427] and z [0.0157, 0.1379], so that
           centre of mass is 14 cm off the mesh in +y and above its top face. Only x
           (0.0754 vs the mesh centre 0.0756) is right, so the export lost the y/z
           frame. It also puts the CoM outside the base's support polygon, so the
           model tips: with a heavier base it fell onto its side (roll -1.589 rad)
           within seconds of spawning.
           Replaced with the collision box centroid: a uniform box is the honest
           approximation, and it is the same box the physics already contacts. -->
      <origin xyz="0.0756 -0.1697 0.0768" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <mass value="1.75590983031249"/>
      <inertia ixx="0.0106901651591523" ixy="1.2533292416581E-05" ixz="-1.78802486116636E-05"
               iyy="0.0121389538778755" iyz="-0.0001726147454493" izz="0.00523087609446992"/>
    </inertial>
    <visual>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry><mesh filename="package://vision_arm_description/meshes/base_link.STL"/></geometry>
      <material name="arm_joint"/>
    </visual>
    <!-- Collision = mesh bounding box, not the STL: DART grinding the 120k-tri
         base mesh against the ground plane every 1ms step (and 278k tris total
         across the arm) held sim RTF at ~0.006. Boxes restore realtime; they're
         conservative for MoveIt planning, which is fine. Jaw STLs stay meshes -
         they're small and need real geometry to grasp. -->
    <collision>
      <origin xyz="0.0757 -0.1697 0.0768" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry><box size="0.1679 0.254 0.1222"/></geometry>
    </collision>
  </link>

  <link name="link1_base_rotate"> <!-- was: rotary_base -->
    <inertial>
      <origin xyz="3.6594E-07 -0.12491 -0.0091222" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <mass value="1.8968"/>
      <inertia ixx="0.011703" ixy="6.2683E-07" ixz="2.7765E-07"
               iyy="0.010005" iyz="-0.00096832" izz="0.0065158"/>
    </inertial>
    <visual>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry><mesh filename="package://vision_arm_description/meshes/link1_base_rotate.STL"/></geometry>
      <material name="arm_body"/>
    </visual>
    <collision>
      <origin xyz="0 -0.127 0.0024" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry><box size="0.1596 0.254 0.2408"/></geometry>
    </collision>
  </link>
  <joint name="joint1_base_rotate" type="revolute"> <!-- was: rotary_base -->
    <origin xyz="0.075629 -0.21266 0.050734" rpy="-1.5708 -6.123E-17 -1.5708"/>
    <parent link="base_link"/>
    <child link="link1_base_rotate"/>
    <axis xyz="0 -1 0"/>
    <limit lower="-1.5708" upper="1.5708" velocity="${vel_limit}" effort="${eff_limit}"/>
  </joint>

  <link name="link2_upper_arm"> <!-- was: nadlaktica -->
    <inertial>
      <origin xyz="0.11187 -0.011704 0.007695" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <mass value="3.097"/>
      <inertia ixx="0.011686" ixy="0.0012432" ixz="-0.0018781"
               iyy="0.03891" iyz="-5.4012E-05" izz="0.032612"/>
    </inertial>
    <visual>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry><mesh filename="package://vision_arm_description/meshes/link2_upper_arm.STL"/></geometry>
      <material name="arm_joint"/>
    </visual>
    <collision>
      <origin xyz="0.1563 -0.0075 -0.0025" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry><box size="0.4175 0.1199 0.1853"/></geometry>
    </collision>
  </link>
  <joint name="joint2_shoulder" type="revolute"> <!-- was: nadlaktica -->
    <origin xyz="0 -0.20182 0" rpy="2.7756E-15 -1.3878E-15 -1.8111"/>
    <parent link="link1_base_rotate"/>
    <child link="link2_upper_arm"/>
    <axis xyz="0 0 1"/>
    <!-- lower=-0.001 (not exactly 0): joint2/joint3 home at their limit switch
         (see the joint-limit comment at the top of this file), so simulated
         home position lands on this bound to within float noise (~1e-14).
         MoveIt's CheckStartStateBounds has no tolerance and rejects a start
         state sitting exactly on/past a hard 0 limit, so both get a hair of
         slack rather than a real range change. -->
    <limit lower="-0.001" upper="3.4907" velocity="${vel_limit}" effort="${eff_limit}"/>
  </joint>

  <link name="link3_elbow"> <!-- was: lakat -->
    <inertial>
      <origin xyz="0.020310022580536 -0.0354589471585284 0.00171229454365077" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <mass value="1.2726491805247"/>
      <inertia ixx="0.00430935066273582" ixy="-0.000472663673983171" ixz="2.10793692092687E-05"
               iyy="0.00388873322685888" iyz="-0.000102922808916268" izz="0.00512549454697952"/>
    </inertial>
    <visual>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry><mesh filename="package://vision_arm_description/meshes/link3_elbow.STL"/></geometry>
      <material name="arm_body"/>
    </visual>
    <collision>
      <origin xyz="0.0464 -0.0335 0.0037" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry><box size="0.1827 0.157 0.1368"/></geometry>
    </collision>
  </link>
  <joint name="joint3_elbow" type="revolute"> <!-- was: lakat -->
    <origin xyz="0.32 0 0" rpy="3.1416 -6.3895E-16 -1.1753"/>
    <parent link="link2_upper_arm"/>
    <child link="link3_elbow"/>
    <axis xyz="0 0 -1"/>
    <limit lower="-0.001" upper="1.8326" velocity="${vel_limit}" effort="${eff_limit}"/>
  </joint>

  <link name="link4_forearm"> <!-- was: podlaktica -->
    <inertial>
      <origin xyz="0.015666 0.10444 0.04524" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <mass value="0.8658"/>
      <inertia ixx="0.0043737" ixy="-0.00033248" ixz="-0.00029637"
               iyy="0.0017275" iyz="-0.0011009" izz="0.0039233"/>
    </inertial>
    <visual>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry><mesh filename="package://vision_arm_description/meshes/link4_forearm.STL"/></geometry>
      <material name="arm_joint"/>
    </visual>
    <collision>
      <origin xyz="0.0183 0.1122 0.0576" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry><box size="0.1403 0.3178 0.1905"/></geometry>
    </collision>
  </link>
  <joint name="joint4_forearm" type="revolute"> <!-- was: podlaktica -->
    <origin xyz="0 -0.0735 0" rpy="-0.5648 -0.78173 1.7809"/>
    <parent link="link3_elbow"/>
    <child link="link4_forearm"/>
    <axis xyz="0.14804 0.90477 0.39934"/>
    <limit lower="-3.0164" upper="2.2872" velocity="${vel_limit}" effort="${eff_limit}"/>
  </joint>

  <link name="link5_wrist"> <!-- was: saka -->
    <inertial>
      <origin xyz="0.00102127447654127 0.00173769308862193 -0.00307332521672926" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <mass value="0.195660541483626"/>
      <inertia ixx="0.000196163267087824" ixy="-1.19915207726741E-07" ixz="-4.99910323817217E-07"
               iyy="0.000195263940589931" iyz="9.07364788265256E-06" izz="0.000136132402412911"/>
    </inertial>
    <visual>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry><mesh filename="package://vision_arm_description/meshes/link5_wrist.STL"/></geometry>
      <material name="arm_body"/>
    </visual>
    <collision>
      <origin xyz="0.0013 0.0071 -0.0041" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry><box size="0.0745 0.0744 0.1018"/></geometry>
    </collision>
  </link>
  <joint name="joint5_wrist" type="revolute"> <!-- was: saka -->
    <origin xyz="0.037114 0.22683 0.10011" rpy="2.2965 -0.74045 -0.57161"/>
    <parent link="link4_forearm"/>
    <child link="link5_wrist"/>
    <axis xyz="-1 0 0"/>
    <limit lower="-1.675" upper="2.0378" velocity="${vel_limit}" effort="${eff_limit}"/>
  </joint>

  <link name="link6_gripper_mount"> <!-- was: hvataljka -->
    <inertial>
      <origin xyz="0.00017222 0.00017758 0.00947" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <mass value="0.020284"/>
      <inertia ixx="3.4622E-06" ixy="-9.927E-08" ixz="-2.2616E-08"
               iyy="3.4561E-06" iyz="-2.332E-08" izz="6.3508E-06"/>
    </inertial>
    <visual>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry><mesh filename="package://vision_arm_description/meshes/link6_gripper_mount.STL"/></geometry>
      <material name="arm_joint"/>
    </visual>
    <collision>
      <origin xyz="0.0002 0.0007 0.008" rpy="0 0 0"/>
      <geometry><box size="0.0574 0.0581 0.0135"/></geometry>
    </collision>
  </link>
  <joint name="joint6_gripper_mount" type="revolute"> <!-- was: hvataljka -->
    <origin xyz="0 0 -0.042312" rpy="-3.1416 2.5362E-15 0.2419"/>
    <parent link="link5_wrist"/>
    <child link="link6_gripper_mount"/>
    <axis xyz="0 0 1"/>
    <limit lower="-3.1416" upper="3.1612" velocity="${vel_limit}" effort="${eff_limit}"/>
  </joint>

  <!-- Camera is the MSG gripper's built-in mount (see gripper.xacro), not a
       separate bracket - the gripper macro below adds camera_link /
       camera_optical_link as children of "gripper". -->
  <xacro:msg_gripper parent_link="link6_gripper_mount"/>

  <ros2_control name="vision_arm_system" type="system">
    <hardware>
      <plugin>gz_ros2_control/GazeboSimSystem</plugin>
    </hardware>
    <xacro:macro name="vision_arm_joint_interface" params="joint">
      <joint name="${joint}">
        <command_interface name="position"/>
        <state_interface name="position"/>
        <state_interface name="velocity"/>
      </joint>
    </xacro:macro>
    <xacro:vision_arm_joint_interface joint="joint1_base_rotate"/>
    <xacro:vision_arm_joint_interface joint="joint2_shoulder"/>
    <xacro:vision_arm_joint_interface joint="joint3_elbow"/>
    <xacro:vision_arm_joint_interface joint="joint4_forearm"/>
    <xacro:vision_arm_joint_interface joint="joint5_wrist"/>
    <xacro:vision_arm_joint_interface joint="joint6_gripper_mount"/>
    <xacro:vision_arm_joint_interface joint="joint_jaw1"/>
    <!-- jaw2 is a real commanded joint, not a mimic: neither gz sim's DART
         build nor gz_ros2_control 1.2 enforces URDF <mimic> (verified: no
         mimic code in libgz_ros2_control-system.so), so the tag was removed
         from gripper.xacro and gripper_controller commands both jaws -
         callers send [jaw1, -jaw1]. -->
    <xacro:vision_arm_joint_interface joint="joint_jaw2"/>
  </ros2_control>

  <gazebo>
    <plugin filename="gz_ros2_control-system" name="gz_ros2_control::GazeboSimROS2ControlPlugin">
      <parameters>$(find vision_arm_gazebo)/config/controllers.yaml</parameters>
    </plugin>
  </gazebo>

  <!-- RGBD sensor at camera_link.

       The <pose> below is REQUIRED and was verified empirically, not assumed.
       An SDF camera sensor looks along its own +X (with +Y left, +Z up) - NOT
       along -Z. A previous comment here claimed Gazebo looks along -Z and
       attached the sensor with no offset; the result was a camera pointing
       horizontally forward while camera_optical_link claimed it pointed down.
       The images were full of gripper and horizon, and any deprojection through
       TF would have placed detections in completely the wrong part of the world.

       Proven by deprojecting a real depth frame through camera_info and
       transforming it by each candidate convention, then checking the result
       against the known scene as it stood then (floor z=0, table top z=0.28;
       the arm has since been bolted to a 0.75 m workbench, which does not change
       the conclusion):

         view = camera_link -Z (the old claim) -> 41.0% of points BELOW the floor,
                                                  0% on floor, 0% on table
         view = camera_link +X (SDF default)   -> 0% below floor,
                                                  41.0% on floor, 10.1% on table

       So the sensor frame must be rotated so its +X lands on our intended view
       direction. Required sensor axes, expressed in camera_link:
         sensor +X (view) = -Z   (camera_link's view direction)
         sensor +Y (left) = -X
         sensor +Z (up)   = +Y
       which is rpy = (-pi/2, +pi/2, 0). That makes the sensor's optical axis
       coincide with camera_optical_link's +Z, so gz_frame_id below is finally
       telling the truth. -->
  <!-- gz_frame_id points at camera_optical_link (the ROS-optical-convention
       child frame) since depth/point-cloud data is expressed in optical axes
       (x-right, y-down, z-forward-along-view). -->
  <!-- Sensor is gated by the same $(arg camera) declared at the top of this file. -->
  <xacro:if value="$(arg camera)">
  <gazebo reference="camera_link">
    <sensor name="wrist_camera" type="rgbd_camera">
      <pose>0 0 0 -1.5707963 1.5707963 0</pose>
      <always_on>true</always_on>
      <!-- ponytail: 320x240@5Hz - this machine software-renders (no dGPU) and
           640x480@15Hz tanked RTF; bump back up if/when run on a GPU box -->
      <update_rate>5</update_rate>
      <visualize>true</visualize>
      <topic>camera</topic>
      <gz_frame_id>camera_optical_link</gz_frame_id>
      <camera>
        <!-- Matched to the real D405 (Intel datasheet 337029-017): depth FOV is
             H 84 / V 58 deg, so horizontal_fov = 84 deg = 1.4661 rad. Note the
             datasheet figure is for 16:9; this sensor renders 4:3, from which
             Gazebo derives vfov ~68 deg rather than the D405's 58 deg. The wider
             simulated vertical is the conservative direction (sim sees at least as
             much as hardware), but do not calibrate against it - use the real
             camera_info once hardware is on. Product pages quoting 87 deg are
             citing the D435/D455; the D405 is 84. -->
        <horizontal_fov>1.4661</horizontal_fov>
        <image>
          <width>320</width>
          <height>240</height>
          <format>R8G8B8</format>
        </image>
        <!-- near = the D405's real 7 cm minimum depth, so the simulated camera goes
             blind at the same distance the hardware does. That is deliberate: it
             makes the "look -> decide -> commit" constraint (description.md 11.4)
             show up in simulation instead of surprising us on hardware. far stays
             at 3 m; the D405's 50 cm figure is its *ideal* range, not a hard cutoff. -->
        <clip>
          <near>0.07</near>
          <far>3.0</far>
        </clip>
      </camera>
    </sensor>
  </gazebo>
  </xacro:if>

</robot>
